      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=-3+t^{4}$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=-3+t^{4}$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(-3+t^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(-3+t^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -3\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -3\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+\\frac{t^{4+1}}{(4+1)}+C \\\\ &= -3t+\\frac{t^{5}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny additive constant in an antiderivative can be absorbed into the displayed arbitrary constant $C$.",
